.TH "SKOGAI RUNNER" 1  "skogai runner 0.0.0" 
.SH NAME
skogai runner \- typed io for handing work to agents: prompt + files \-> json matching a schema
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskogai runner\fR <COMMAND>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.br
typed io for handing work to agents: prompt + files \-> json matching a schema
.br

.br
pure by default: only `\-\-apply` changes existing files, scaffolds only create new ones.
.br
file arguments are relative to the current directory; workorders, the journal
.br
and logs live in the local .skogai.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
skogai runner\-send(1)
send a prompt and files to an agent, print its typed output

the prompt comes from \-\-prompt or stdin. files are inlined as
<file path="..."> blocks, so the agent needs no tools to read them.
with \-\-schema stdout is the json object, without it plain text.
.TP
skogai runner\-improve(1)
ask an agent to edit files, print the patch. pure unless \-\-apply

the agent returns whole files (schema `edit`), the patch is derived
here with `diff \-u`, so a bad hunk header can never break it.
.TP
skogai runner\-wo(1)
workorders: one bounded question, gathered inputs, checkable output
.TP
skogai runner\-journal(1)
session journals (runner.journal in .skogai): read them back, harvest them
